{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Every good problem statement ends with a **constraints** section, and most beginners skip it. That's a mistake: the constraints quietly tell you how fast your solution needs to be — and therefore which approaches are even worth trying."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The rule of thumb: about 10⁸ simple steps per second"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A typical judge (and a typical interviewer's expectation) allows roughly **one second**, and a computer does somewhere around **100 million** simple operations in that time. So plug the largest `n` into your algorithm's complexity and check whether the result stays under about 10⁸."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "n = 100,000 (10⁵)\n\nO(n²)       → 10¹⁰ steps     ✗  about 100 seconds: too slow\nO(n log n)  → 1.7 × 10⁶      ✓\nO(n)        → 10⁵            ✓"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "From n to the complexity you need"
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/cf675d8e-3517-480a-8b97-56b4539f4f10.svg",
      "alt": "A table from input size to the complexity that fits in about a second: n up to 10 allows O(n!); up to 20 allows O(2 to the n); up to 500 allows O(n cubed); up to 5,000 allows O(n squared); up to a million allows O(n log n) or O(n); larger needs O(log n) or O(1).",
      "width": 780,
      "height": 536,
