    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Most array problems you'll meet have `n` up to 10⁵ or 10⁶, which means the brute force O(n²) is too slow and you're expected to find something **O(n)** or **O(n log n)**. When `n` is small, the problem is telling you that an exponential search is fine — often because the problem is genuinely hard otherwise."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Read the value constraints too"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "It isn't just `n`. The **values** change which technique works:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Can values be negative?** Negatives break sliding windows on sums (adding an element can make the sum smaller) — see Kadane's algorithm and prefix sums instead.",
        "**Can values be zero?** Zeros break \"divide by the total\" tricks — see Product of array except self.",
        "**Is the array sorted?** Then two pointers or binary search are on the table.",
        "**Are values in a small range, like 1 to n?** Then an array can replace a hash map, or the index-as-address tricks of **Cyclic sort** apply.",
        "**How big can the answer get?** If sums can exceed about 2.1 × 10⁹, use `long` in Java and C++.",
        "**Are duplicates allowed?** Duplicates are the most common source of wrong answers — decide early how they should be counted."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Worked example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "Problem:     find two numbers in nums that add up to target.\nConstraints: 2 ≤ n ≤ 10⁴,  -10⁹ ≤ nums[i] ≤ 10⁹"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "`n ≤ 10⁴` → O(n²) is about 10⁸ steps: **borderline**, likely accepted, but an interviewer will ask for better.",
        "Values up to 10⁹ in size → you can't use them as array indexes (far too big), so a counting array is out; a **hash map** is the tool for \"have I seen the complement?\".",
        "Two values up to 10⁹ can sum to 2 × 10⁹ → that doesn't fit in a Java/C++ `int`. Use `long` for the sum."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Two lines of constraints, and you already know the intended solution is **O(n) with a hash map** — which is exactly the **Two Sum** solution in the Hash map chapter."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Make it a habit"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Before writing any code, say out loud: \"n is up to ___, so I need about ___ or better. Values can / can't be negative, sorted / not sorted.\" Ten seconds of reading saves you from building a beautiful solution that is too slow."
    }
  ],
